 Small operations repository for the log retention job.
 
-`bin/archive-logs.sh SERVICE` packs the logs of one service from `logs/` into a dated
-tarball under `archives/`. It runs from cron on the log host every night.
+## Usage
 
-Archives are pruned by hand today.
+```
+bin/archive-logs.sh [--keep-days N] SERVICE [SERVICE...]
+```
+
+`bin/archive-logs.sh` packs the logs of each named service from `logs/` into a dated
+tarball (`SERVICE-YYYYMMDD.tar.gz`, UTC date) under `archives/`. It runs from cron on the
+log host every night, from the repository root (`logs/` and `archives/` are relative paths).
+
+- Several services can be given in one run: `bin/archive-logs.sh web api`.
+- `--keep-days N` (or `--keep-days=N`) removes archives older than N days, by file
+  modification time, after all new archives are in place. Only regular files named
+  `SERVICE-YYYYMMDD.tar.gz` directly inside `archives/` are considered; anything else is
+  left alone. Without the option nothing is removed.
+- On success it prints one summary line, for example:
+  `archived 2 service(s): web api; pruned 3 archive(s) older than 30 day(s)`.
+
+Service names must match `^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9_-]{0,63}$`, and all names are checked before any
+work starts. If any service fails to archive, the script exits non-zero and does not prune.
+
+Exit status: 0 success, 64 usage error, 65 invalid service name or `--keep-days` value,
+anything else is a failure from `tar` or another command.
+
+Requires Bash 4.4 or later and GNU `find`/`date`.
